WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.600
KIRSTY BENNETT: And welcome
to the McGovern Institute's

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.466
first-ever Story Slam.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.466
I'm Kirsty Bennett,
founder of MITell,

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.066
a storytelling program for MIT.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.066
I'll be your moderator
for the next hour or so,

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.733
as we hear seven McGovern
storytellers share true stories

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.800
on the theme of resilience.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.933
We are thrilled to host this
event as part of the McGovern's

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.333
20th anniversary
celebration, and we

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.200
are particularly
delighted to have

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.066
the institute's co-founder,
Lore Harp McGovern,

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.600
in our virtual audience.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.000
Welcome.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.600
I've always been a fan of
the McGovern, even from afar,

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.800
so much so that I
brought both of my babies

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.200
to have their
brains scanned there

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.266
in the name of
scientific research.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.266
They're still only one and
four, but maybe they'll

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.200
be brain scientists,
too, one day.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.333
But in working on
tonight's program,

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.133
it opened up so much more
of the McGovern to me,

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.133
and I know it will for you, too.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.266
Through the power
of these stories,

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.400
you're about to discover
the diversity of discipline

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.933
at the institute, and really
get to know the scientists that

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.000
call it home.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.200
That's right, we are
putting scientists right out

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.133
of their comfort zone and asking
them to talk about themselves,

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.466
as well as their work.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.400
And to our theme
tonight, resilience,

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.066
I think it's fair to say
that everyone watching

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.266
this event tonight, wherever
you are in the world,

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.133
knows something
about resilience.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.533
It's been quite a year.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.333
So tonight, we're
going to celebrate

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.666
that very human strength.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.600
This hour, you will hear
stories of failed experiments,

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.866
of crushing setbacks,
of overwhelming doubt.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.666
These seven storytellers
have experienced

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.333
and overcome it all.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.866
Tonight, each one will share
a true personal story of grit,

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.933
perseverance, and resilience.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.400
We have two judges
for today's slam--

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.666
Maddie Sofia, host of
NPR's daily science

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.266
podcast Short Wave.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.000
There she is.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.533
And Tina Hesman Saey, a
senior writer at Science News.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.000
Hello, Tina.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.866
Maddie and Tina will
have the difficult task

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.466
of selecting a winner
for today's slam

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.533
at the end of the hour.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.466
They will judge each
five-minute story

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.733
on the following criteria.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.333
One, theme-- how
well does it relate

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.800
to the theme of resilience?

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.666
Two, clarity-- is the story,
and any scientific concepts

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.133
in the story, easy
to understand?

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.400
And originality-- is the
story uniquely captivating,

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.333
and is it memorable?

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.866
The winner will receive a cash
reward, this amazing trophy,

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.266
and a giant McGovern swag
bag filled with goodies.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.333
And of course, the
prestige of being

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.200
named the winner
of the first ever

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.000
McGovern Institute Story Slam.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.066
A few quick housekeeping notes.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.933
This event is being recorded.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.200
If you like what
you hear tonight,

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.800
please tag us and help us
spread the word on social media.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.333
And our hashtag is
McGovern Stories.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.533
Without further ado,
let's get started.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.266
Now, to introduce
our storytellers,

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.866
I asked them all a question.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.600
And I asked them, if you
could go back in time to March

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.200
and stockpile
anything, absolutely

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.800
anything, what would it be?

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.266
When I asked our
first storyteller,

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.266
Ola Ozernov-Palchik,
this question,

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.866
she answered post-testing fMRI
data for her study and stock

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.000
in Zoom.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.266
Please welcome Ola.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.333
OLA OZERNOV-PALCHIK:
I was nine when

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.600
my family made the journey
from the former Soviet Union

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.000
to Israel.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.133
We arrived with four overflowing
suitcases and one broken floor

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.000
lamp.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.733
Immigrants have to make rational
decisions on which items

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.733
to bring on thier journey.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.200
For my family, it
was a floor lamp

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.933
that we carried with us through
two countries on our journey

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.400
to the promised land.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.800
For my husband's family, it
was two Soviet tennis rackets.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.733
When we left Russia, we left
behind our family, our home,

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.533
and our identity, but we had
a lot to look forward to.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.133
My parents were
hopeful to escape

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.266
the poverty and anti-Semitism
of the Soviet Union.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.862
My sister and I were
excited about all the things

00:00:00.862 --> 00:00:05.392
that the Western world had
to offer, mostly bubblegum.

00:00:05.392 --> 00:00:07.912
We settled in a small and
government-furnished apartment,

00:00:07.912 --> 00:00:09.742
and I anxiously
awaited for the boxes

00:00:09.742 --> 00:00:11.872
we shipped from
Russia to arrive.

00:00:11.872 --> 00:00:14.132
My books were in these boxes.

00:00:14.132 --> 00:00:16.132
I was an avid reader as
a child, and although we

00:00:16.132 --> 00:00:17.842
didn't have much,
I remember always

00:00:17.842 --> 00:00:20.812
being very proud of the
wall-to-wall shelves of books

00:00:20.812 --> 00:00:22.342
my family had.

00:00:22.342 --> 00:00:26.031
The books allowed me to escape
my narrow world of long food

00:00:26.031 --> 00:00:28.312
lines for the magnificent
worlds experienced

00:00:28.312 --> 00:00:30.982
by the heroes in my books.

00:00:30.982 --> 00:00:34.612
Our boxes arrived three
months later, mostly intact,

00:00:34.612 --> 00:00:37.252
except for two things that
didn't make the journey--

00:00:37.252 --> 00:00:40.972
a folder full of my art
and the box of my books.

00:00:40.972 --> 00:00:42.982
As for my art, I
grudgingly accepted

00:00:42.982 --> 00:00:45.862
my parents' explanation
that my art was confiscated

00:00:45.862 --> 00:00:48.232
by the authorities
because it represented

00:00:48.232 --> 00:00:51.922
valuable cultural artifacts
forbidden to leave Russia.

00:00:51.922 --> 00:00:56.032
One of these days, I'm sure
I'll find them in the Hermitage.

00:00:56.032 --> 00:00:58.282
I was devastated by
the loss of my books.

00:00:58.282 --> 00:01:01.132
We were among the early wave
of the mass migration of Soviet

00:01:01.132 --> 00:01:03.812
Jews to Israel, and
when we arrived,

00:01:03.812 --> 00:01:06.292
there were no Russian language
books to be found anywhere,

00:01:06.292 --> 00:01:09.392
and so I was left bookless.

00:01:09.392 --> 00:01:11.342
The irony of the Russian
immigrants to Israel

00:01:11.342 --> 00:01:13.142
is that, in Russia, we
were never Russians.

00:01:13.142 --> 00:01:14.312
We were Jews.

00:01:14.312 --> 00:01:16.592
It said so in our
identity cards.

00:01:16.592 --> 00:01:18.872
In Israel, however, we
were never Israelis.

00:01:18.872 --> 00:01:20.372
We were Russian.

00:01:20.372 --> 00:01:22.892
The reality that I will never
truly belong in this new

00:01:22.892 --> 00:01:26.042
country became evident to
me within the first few days

00:01:26.042 --> 00:01:28.682
in my new school when I arrived
to school wearing a traditional

00:01:28.682 --> 00:01:32.202
Russian first-day-of-school
dress with bows in my hair,

00:01:32.202 --> 00:01:34.742
just to find everyone
else dressed comfortably

00:01:34.742 --> 00:01:36.752
in sweatpants.

00:01:36.752 --> 00:01:38.972
I was desperate to belong,
and mastering the language

00:01:38.972 --> 00:01:40.772
was my entry ticket.

00:01:40.772 --> 00:01:42.062
At first, I had a slow start.

00:01:42.062 --> 00:01:44.072
For my first few months
we were in Israel,

00:01:44.072 --> 00:01:46.892
my mom paid me $0.05 per
page to motivate me to read

00:01:46.892 --> 00:01:48.602
children's picture books.

00:01:48.602 --> 00:01:50.432
I went from reading
Around the World

00:01:50.432 --> 00:01:53.852
in 80 Days in Russian
to laboriously decoding

00:01:53.852 --> 00:01:58.142
Llama Llama Red Pajama in
Hebrew one word at a time.

00:01:58.142 --> 00:02:01.112
It was painful and demoralizing,
and I felt like a bird

00:02:01.112 --> 00:02:02.312
without wings.

00:02:02.312 --> 00:02:03.962
I was ready to pack
up my belongings

00:02:03.962 --> 00:02:07.322
and travel back to Russia
in pursuit of my lost art.

00:02:07.322 --> 00:02:09.045
But the week before
summer vacation,

00:02:09.045 --> 00:02:10.712
the class took a trip
to a local library

00:02:10.712 --> 00:02:12.542
to pick out their
summer readings,

00:02:12.542 --> 00:02:14.402
and I came across
a book about a boy

00:02:14.402 --> 00:02:19.012
in a foster family that was a
part of an eight-book series.

00:02:19.012 --> 00:02:21.792
I was immediately sucked in,
and by the end of the summer,

00:02:21.792 --> 00:02:24.842
I finished all the books in the
series, and by September 1st,

00:02:24.842 --> 00:02:27.992
I was so fluent in Hebrew that
I was exempt from the language

00:02:27.992 --> 00:02:30.422
learners program at my school.

00:02:30.422 --> 00:02:32.912
Reading was my
anchor and my outlet

00:02:32.912 --> 00:02:35.582
through our many moves
over the following years.

00:02:35.582 --> 00:02:37.172
Through my very
rough adolescence,

00:02:37.172 --> 00:02:38.912
books of existential
philosophers

00:02:38.912 --> 00:02:42.272
gave me a grand perspective
on my teenage angst.

00:02:42.272 --> 00:02:44.042
In the small and
isolated kibbutz

00:02:44.042 --> 00:02:45.932
where I was sent to
escape the bad influences

00:02:45.932 --> 00:02:48.632
of my neighborhood, in the
remote military base where

00:02:48.632 --> 00:02:51.992
I served for two years, and
in my early days in the US,

00:02:51.992 --> 00:02:53.852
when I began attending
community college,

00:02:53.852 --> 00:02:57.782
my reading is what helped
me survive, and even thrive.

00:02:57.782 --> 00:02:59.552
Reading was everything
to me, and so I

00:02:59.552 --> 00:03:01.162
was struck when I
first encountered

00:03:01.162 --> 00:03:03.360
a child who couldn't read.

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:05.402
Given my passion for
reading, it's not surprising

00:03:05.402 --> 00:03:07.027
that, during my junior
year of college,

00:03:07.027 --> 00:03:09.662
I began working at the
Institute for Reading Research.

00:03:09.662 --> 00:03:12.092
I evaluated the efficacy
of a curriculum developed

00:03:12.092 --> 00:03:13.412
by the institute researchers.

00:03:13.412 --> 00:03:15.542
And through this work,
I would regularly

00:03:15.542 --> 00:03:18.280
visit schools in wealthy
suburban, inner city,

00:03:18.280 --> 00:03:20.822
and immigrant neighborhoods to
administer reading assessments

00:03:20.822 --> 00:03:23.372
to children at
regular intervals.

00:03:23.372 --> 00:03:25.922
I was struck at the variability
with which children made

00:03:25.922 --> 00:03:28.292
progress from month to month.

00:03:28.292 --> 00:03:30.212
Some kids could
read effortlessly,

00:03:30.212 --> 00:03:32.042
others with a bit
more difficulty,

00:03:32.042 --> 00:03:34.442
and then there were
children like Rubin,

00:03:34.442 --> 00:03:36.992
who had developmental dyslexia.

00:03:36.992 --> 00:03:39.422
Rubin was a bright and
chatty second grader

00:03:39.422 --> 00:03:42.332
who in the span of our walk
down the hall from his classroom

00:03:42.332 --> 00:03:45.842
to the testing room would not
only update me on his latest

00:03:45.842 --> 00:03:49.112
LEGO creations, but also
the most intimate details

00:03:49.112 --> 00:03:52.622
of his family's life, like
his Aunt Rachel's divorce.

00:03:52.622 --> 00:03:54.812
We would enter the
testing room, and his face

00:03:54.812 --> 00:03:56.612
would visibly fall.

00:03:56.612 --> 00:03:59.402
Rubin would sit in front of me
every two weeks, frustrated,

00:03:59.402 --> 00:04:01.532
with tears welling
up in his eyes,

00:04:01.532 --> 00:04:05.702
trying his hardest to read
through a passage word

00:04:05.702 --> 00:04:10.682
by word by word, just like
I did so many years ago.

00:04:10.682 --> 00:04:13.502
But for him, this wasn't
a fleeting hiccup,

00:04:13.502 --> 00:04:17.172
but the beginning of
a lifelong struggle.

00:04:17.172 --> 00:04:18.842
This experience
sparked my passion

00:04:18.842 --> 00:04:21.602
to unravel the neural mechanisms
of individual differences

00:04:21.602 --> 00:04:23.322
in reading development.

00:04:23.322 --> 00:04:25.152
In my research here
at McGovern Institute,

00:04:25.152 --> 00:04:26.652
we've been discovering
that children

00:04:26.652 --> 00:04:28.412
at risk for
developmental dyslexia

00:04:28.412 --> 00:04:31.892
show brain alterations often
associated with this disorder

00:04:31.892 --> 00:04:35.192
as early as first few
months of kindergarten.

00:04:35.192 --> 00:04:37.662
Despite substantial hereditary
and behavioral risk,

00:04:37.662 --> 00:04:40.592
however, some children proceed
to develop typical reading

00:04:40.592 --> 00:04:43.262
skills, and the research shows
that these children often

00:04:43.262 --> 00:04:45.301
recruit alternative
neural pathways

00:04:45.301 --> 00:04:48.652
and use different cognitive
strategies for reading.

00:04:48.652 --> 00:04:51.902
So I'm interested in figuring
out these mechanisms of risk

00:04:51.902 --> 00:04:53.972
and resilience in
reading development

00:04:53.972 --> 00:04:55.802
because I believe that
reading is not only

00:04:55.802 --> 00:04:57.632
a privilege and an
anchor, as it was

00:04:57.632 --> 00:05:02.392
for me, but a basic human right
that all children should enjoy.

00:05:02.392 --> 00:05:02.892
Thank you.

00:05:06.756 --> 00:05:08.462
KIRSTY BENNETT: Yay to Ola.

00:05:08.462 --> 00:05:11.362
Thank you so much for being
our first storyteller.

00:05:11.362 --> 00:05:15.012
I love that story, and I
have such a clear image

00:05:15.012 --> 00:05:19.272
of the determined little girl
who loves bubblegum and wants

00:05:19.272 --> 00:05:20.962
to find a way to read.

00:05:20.962 --> 00:05:25.402
Thank you for bringing her
into the room with you tonight.

00:05:25.402 --> 00:05:29.042
Our next storyteller
is Jill Crittenden.

00:05:29.042 --> 00:05:31.319
And when I asked Jill
what she would stockpile

00:05:31.319 --> 00:05:32.902
if she could go back
in time to March,

00:05:32.902 --> 00:05:35.482
she answered stock in Zoom.

00:05:35.482 --> 00:05:38.332
But, she added, she is
not ready to tell us what

00:05:38.332 --> 00:05:39.922
she would do with the profits.

00:05:39.922 --> 00:05:42.542
Please welcome Jill.

00:05:42.542 --> 00:05:45.322
JILL CRITTENDEN: Thank you.

00:05:45.322 --> 00:05:48.002
Perseverance is usually
considered a good thing.

00:05:48.002 --> 00:05:51.307
It takes perseverance to learn
skills like riding a bike.

00:05:51.307 --> 00:05:52.682
Every time you
fall off, you have

00:05:52.682 --> 00:05:55.502
to get back on again and again.

00:05:55.502 --> 00:05:57.932
In fact, if you've ever
had dinner with a toddler,

00:05:57.932 --> 00:06:00.572
you know that even a simple
behavior like feeding yourself

00:06:00.572 --> 00:06:03.212
takes perseverance
and repetition.

00:06:03.212 --> 00:06:04.802
After a while,
the whole sequence

00:06:04.802 --> 00:06:07.502
of movements required for
whatever task you're doing

00:06:07.502 --> 00:06:11.342
seems effortless, and
we just go on autopilot.

00:06:11.342 --> 00:06:14.312
But sometimes, that autopilot
is our own worst enemy,

00:06:14.312 --> 00:06:16.772
and unconsciously
initiates harmful habits,

00:06:16.772 --> 00:06:19.762
like nail-biting or smoking.

00:06:19.762 --> 00:06:22.892
In many brain disorders, such
as autism and schizophrenia,

00:06:22.892 --> 00:06:24.752
repetitive behaviors
can be severe

00:06:24.752 --> 00:06:27.522
and very difficult to disrupt.

00:06:27.522 --> 00:06:31.572
People become so stuck doing the
same thing over and over again

00:06:31.572 --> 00:06:33.222
that they seem
completely unaware

00:06:33.222 --> 00:06:35.592
of the world around them.

00:06:35.592 --> 00:06:38.022
A few years ago, I was
studying the biological basis

00:06:38.022 --> 00:06:40.842
of these extreme repetitive
behaviors in Ann Graybiel's

00:06:40.842 --> 00:06:43.302
lab, and we made a
remarkable discovery

00:06:43.302 --> 00:06:47.772
about why they might sometimes
be so hard to disrupt.

00:06:47.772 --> 00:06:50.122
That's interesting
in and of itself,

00:06:50.122 --> 00:06:51.852
but the resilience
part of my story

00:06:51.852 --> 00:06:56.362
is about what happened when we
tried to publish our discovery.

00:06:56.362 --> 00:06:59.122
We were doing experiments to
test which neurons in the brain

00:06:59.122 --> 00:07:00.502
to disrupt a habit.

00:07:00.502 --> 00:07:02.182
For example, if
someone says stop

00:07:02.182 --> 00:07:06.442
biting your nails, which neurons
here it and make you stop?

00:07:06.442 --> 00:07:09.212
We were using genetically
engineered mice to study this

00:07:09.212 --> 00:07:10.672
so that we could
actually control

00:07:10.672 --> 00:07:12.832
the activity of
specific brain cells

00:07:12.832 --> 00:07:16.462
to figure out which ones
disrupt the autopilot.

00:07:16.462 --> 00:07:19.612
To induce the repetitive
behaviors, we used amphetamine.

00:07:19.612 --> 00:07:22.942
You might or might not know
that amphetamine causes really

00:07:22.942 --> 00:07:24.592
extreme repetitive
behaviors that

00:07:24.592 --> 00:07:28.942
are really hard to disrupt,
like skin picking or pacing.

00:07:28.942 --> 00:07:32.722
Our big discovery was that these
extreme repetitive behaviors

00:07:32.722 --> 00:07:35.392
were not interruptible
like they should be.

00:07:35.392 --> 00:07:37.642
The autopilot was
deaf, and could not

00:07:37.642 --> 00:07:41.932
be turned off when you were
exposed to amphetamine.

00:07:41.932 --> 00:07:44.782
Our discovery suggested
that, in other cases

00:07:44.782 --> 00:07:47.612
of pathological repetitive
behaviors, when people

00:07:47.612 --> 00:07:51.592
were very engaged and seem to
not even hear the people who

00:07:51.592 --> 00:07:54.252
are trying to interrupt
them, in fact,

00:07:54.252 --> 00:07:56.452
the habit part of their
brain might not even

00:07:56.452 --> 00:07:58.132
be getting the
signal that somebody

00:07:58.132 --> 00:08:00.734
is trying to talk to them.

00:08:00.734 --> 00:08:02.692
When we were writing up
this exciting discovery

00:08:02.692 --> 00:08:07.132
for publication, kaboom, a
Canadian group unexpectedly

00:08:07.132 --> 00:08:10.732
published a paper about the
genetically engineered mice

00:08:10.732 --> 00:08:13.792
we had used, and they
said that these mice, when

00:08:13.792 --> 00:08:15.832
they were trying to
solve a task in a maze,

00:08:15.832 --> 00:08:19.776
kept turning the wrong
way over and over again.

00:08:19.776 --> 00:08:20.602
Ugh.

00:08:20.602 --> 00:08:22.432
What did that mean
about the discovery

00:08:22.432 --> 00:08:24.202
we'd made about
repetitive behaviors

00:08:24.202 --> 00:08:27.352
using these very same mice?

00:08:27.352 --> 00:08:29.332
Our team had an
emergency meeting,

00:08:29.332 --> 00:08:31.672
and we figured out how
to edit our manuscript

00:08:31.672 --> 00:08:33.322
and incorporate the
Canadian group's

00:08:33.322 --> 00:08:35.372
findings into our paper.

00:08:35.372 --> 00:08:40.092
And then we sent it to a
top journal, and we waited.

00:08:40.092 --> 00:08:42.942
Let me tell you a bit about
the manuscript review process.

00:08:42.942 --> 00:08:44.902
It has multiple steps.

00:08:44.902 --> 00:08:46.662
The first one is to
interest the journal

00:08:46.662 --> 00:08:48.462
editors enough to
send your paper out

00:08:48.462 --> 00:08:50.862
for review by other scientists.

00:08:50.862 --> 00:08:53.322
We succeeded with that step.

00:08:53.322 --> 00:08:55.662
The second hurdle
is peer review,

00:08:55.662 --> 00:08:58.422
when experts in your field
critique your manuscript

00:08:58.422 --> 00:09:00.162
line by line.

00:09:00.162 --> 00:09:03.042
There are often only two
reviewers of your manuscript,

00:09:03.042 --> 00:09:05.652
but a third reviewer might
be added as a tiebreaker

00:09:05.652 --> 00:09:07.932
if the first two don't
see eye-to-eye, so

00:09:07.932 --> 00:09:10.482
reviewer number three
is the term that's

00:09:10.482 --> 00:09:13.322
often used to the most
harsh critic of your work.

00:09:15.882 --> 00:09:19.302
Finally, our reviews were
back, and we had three of them.

00:09:19.302 --> 00:09:21.882
I carefully read through
the requested changes

00:09:21.882 --> 00:09:24.492
to our manuscript
line by line, but when

00:09:24.492 --> 00:09:28.152
I got to reviewer number
three, my heart sank.

00:09:28.152 --> 00:09:30.342
Number three said that
what the Canadian group had

00:09:30.342 --> 00:09:33.072
said about the genetically
engineered mice that we'd used

00:09:33.072 --> 00:09:35.292
was a deal-breaker,
and that we had

00:09:35.292 --> 00:09:38.172
to repeat all of our
experiments with a completely

00:09:38.172 --> 00:09:40.572
different method.

00:09:40.572 --> 00:09:43.182
This would take
many, many months.

00:09:43.182 --> 00:09:45.552
Moreover, a key
member of our team

00:09:45.552 --> 00:09:49.397
was just about to move
away to California.

00:09:49.397 --> 00:09:50.772
I felt like reviewer
number three

00:09:50.772 --> 00:09:55.182
had broken both my
kneecaps, but I found out

00:09:55.182 --> 00:09:59.412
I could still stand back
up and go in the lab.

00:09:59.412 --> 00:10:01.722
While we set up the new
experimental methods

00:10:01.722 --> 00:10:04.362
and looked for a
replacement team member,

00:10:04.362 --> 00:10:06.252
we looked more closely
at the behavioral mice

00:10:06.252 --> 00:10:08.082
we'd decided to
use, and we found

00:10:08.082 --> 00:10:09.852
that their repetitive
behaviors, in fact,

00:10:09.852 --> 00:10:13.002
went on much longer
than in normal mice.

00:10:13.002 --> 00:10:16.212
And we found that the
genetic mutation in them

00:10:16.212 --> 00:10:17.922
actually might be
related to this

00:10:17.922 --> 00:10:20.892
in the habit part of the
brain, how gene expression had

00:10:20.892 --> 00:10:21.912
changed.

00:10:21.912 --> 00:10:23.412
So this was
interesting because it

00:10:23.412 --> 00:10:25.842
shows how a genetic
mutation can make

00:10:25.842 --> 00:10:28.902
someone more vulnerable to
have these extreme repetitive

00:10:28.902 --> 00:10:30.342
behaviors.

00:10:30.342 --> 00:10:32.622
We wrote up the story
and we published it

00:10:32.622 --> 00:10:34.560
without much trouble.

00:10:34.560 --> 00:10:37.102
Eventually, we were able to get
the requested new experiments

00:10:37.102 --> 00:10:39.442
done, as well, and we
published our discovery

00:10:39.442 --> 00:10:43.282
about how amphetamine can make
the habit-driving autopilot

00:10:43.282 --> 00:10:45.322
numb to inputs.

00:10:45.322 --> 00:10:50.062
So in the end, we got two
papers out of the rejection

00:10:50.062 --> 00:10:52.342
by reviewer number three.

00:10:52.342 --> 00:10:55.822
So when you read that
review, be resilient.

00:10:55.822 --> 00:10:57.142
Persevere.

00:10:57.142 --> 00:10:59.092
Maybe you can make
lemonade out of lemons.

00:11:04.132 --> 00:11:05.882
KIRSTY BENNETT: And a
huge congratulations

00:11:05.882 --> 00:11:07.802
to our second storyteller.

00:11:07.802 --> 00:11:08.852
Thank you so much.

00:11:08.852 --> 00:11:11.372
This is a story I love
because it lets us

00:11:11.372 --> 00:11:14.282
in on the inside
baseball of science

00:11:14.282 --> 00:11:15.772
and the scientific process.

00:11:15.772 --> 00:11:18.092
I know that there will be
many in our audience who

00:11:18.092 --> 00:11:22.382
understand just how terrifying
reviewer number three can be.

00:11:22.382 --> 00:11:25.772
So thank you so much, Jill.

00:11:25.772 --> 00:11:29.222
Our next storyteller
is Kristy Johnson.

00:11:29.222 --> 00:11:31.180
And when I asked what
she would have stockpiled

00:11:31.180 --> 00:11:32.764
if she could go back
in time to March,

00:11:32.764 --> 00:11:34.742
she gave an answer very,
very dear to my heart.

00:11:34.742 --> 00:11:38.132
She said she would have
stockpiled infinite childcare.

00:11:38.132 --> 00:11:39.287
Please welcome Kristy.

00:11:42.852 --> 00:11:44.352
KRISTY JOHNSON: You
know that moment

00:11:44.352 --> 00:11:46.842
in movies where the
character stares off

00:11:46.842 --> 00:11:49.542
into space and the camera
zooms in through their eyeball

00:11:49.542 --> 00:11:52.842
and magically flashes up all
the memories, all the moments

00:11:52.842 --> 00:11:56.772
in their life that led up to
that one particular moment?

00:11:56.772 --> 00:11:59.952
That was me on March 8th, 2012.

00:11:59.952 --> 00:12:03.732
I was in Indiana, sitting on
the beige carpet of my parent's

00:12:03.732 --> 00:12:04.647
living room.

00:12:04.647 --> 00:12:07.077
My best friend's father had
just passed away suddenly,

00:12:07.077 --> 00:12:08.952
and I'd flown home with
my nine-month-old son

00:12:08.952 --> 00:12:10.422
to be with her.

00:12:10.422 --> 00:12:13.632
While I was on the plane, my
son's neurologist had called.

00:12:13.632 --> 00:12:15.762
He left a message
saying you need

00:12:15.762 --> 00:12:18.552
to come in, which is
never a great thing

00:12:18.552 --> 00:12:20.630
to hear from a neurologist.

00:12:20.630 --> 00:12:22.422
The month before, we'd
been referred to him

00:12:22.422 --> 00:12:27.852
because my son, Felix, had
missed every single milestone.

00:12:27.852 --> 00:12:29.089
He wasn't rolling over.

00:12:29.089 --> 00:12:30.672
He couldn't sit,
didn't babble, didn't

00:12:30.672 --> 00:12:35.562
use his hands for anything, not
to grab a toy or touch my face.

00:12:35.562 --> 00:12:37.392
By the time we saw
the neurologist,

00:12:37.392 --> 00:12:40.375
he immediately called
for genetic testing.

00:12:40.375 --> 00:12:42.792
So while I was at the funeral
for my best friend's father,

00:12:42.792 --> 00:12:44.652
my husband was
back in California,

00:12:44.652 --> 00:12:47.292
where we were living at the
time, listening to the doctor

00:12:47.292 --> 00:12:51.222
tell him that Felix was
missing a handful of genes.

00:12:51.222 --> 00:12:55.312
One gene, in particular, was
critical for development,

00:12:55.312 --> 00:12:57.102
and he didn't have it.

00:12:57.102 --> 00:13:00.342
There were seven known cases
worldwide at that time,

00:13:00.342 --> 00:13:03.552
but specific patterns had
already begun to emerge.

00:13:03.552 --> 00:13:06.442
All of these children
had absent speech,

00:13:06.442 --> 00:13:09.042
meaning no spoken words ever.

00:13:09.042 --> 00:13:11.682
They had severe intellectual
disabilities, epilepsy,

00:13:11.682 --> 00:13:13.307
autistic traits,
motor challenges.

00:13:13.307 --> 00:13:14.682
At that time,
there were no cases

00:13:14.682 --> 00:13:16.422
of individuals who could walk.

00:13:16.422 --> 00:13:19.272
They struggled to use their
hands and plan movements.

00:13:19.272 --> 00:13:21.471
And it would be lifelong.

00:13:21.471 --> 00:13:24.721
And that night, as my husband
relayed this information to me

00:13:24.721 --> 00:13:29.221
over the phone, as I was
already exhausted and raw

00:13:29.221 --> 00:13:33.362
and overwhelmed by the
capriciousness of life

00:13:33.362 --> 00:13:37.892
and death, I felt an
extraordinary sensation

00:13:37.892 --> 00:13:39.872
of clarity.

00:13:39.872 --> 00:13:42.992
That was the moment when
all the pieces of my life

00:13:42.992 --> 00:13:47.342
fast forwarded to me sitting
on the beige carpeted corner

00:13:47.342 --> 00:13:49.322
of the living room.

00:13:49.322 --> 00:13:51.332
Up until that
moment, I had thought

00:13:51.332 --> 00:13:53.732
I was going to be
an astrophysicist.

00:13:53.732 --> 00:13:55.652
Before getting married
and having Felix,

00:13:55.652 --> 00:13:58.412
I'd been on a relatively
straight academic path

00:13:58.412 --> 00:13:59.124
in physics.

00:13:59.124 --> 00:14:00.332
I'd been a Goldwater scholar.

00:14:00.332 --> 00:14:02.612
I had graduated summa cum
laude with honors, minors, math

00:14:02.612 --> 00:14:04.070
and astronomy, all
the usual stuff.

00:14:04.070 --> 00:14:07.612
I'd gone straight into a PhD
program out of undergrad.

00:14:07.612 --> 00:14:11.322
But none of that
mattered anymore.

00:14:11.322 --> 00:14:15.392
So instead of returning to
finish my PhD, I stayed home.

00:14:15.392 --> 00:14:17.642
In the evenings, I
taught college astronomy

00:14:17.642 --> 00:14:19.892
and edited scientific
manuscripts and grants

00:14:19.892 --> 00:14:20.972
to pay the bills.

00:14:20.972 --> 00:14:23.792
But when Felix was
awake, I was with him.

00:14:23.792 --> 00:14:27.175
And in every spare second, I was
reading about autism, genetics,

00:14:27.175 --> 00:14:28.592
neuroscience,
anything I could get

00:14:28.592 --> 00:14:30.642
my hands on that might help.

00:14:30.642 --> 00:14:34.112
I began tinkering with Arduinos,
fabricating toys and tools.

00:14:34.112 --> 00:14:37.472
It was clear that, if I wanted
a better world for Felix, more

00:14:37.472 --> 00:14:41.582
knowledge, better tools,
deeper understanding,

00:14:41.582 --> 00:14:45.272
I was going to have
to build it myself.

00:14:45.272 --> 00:14:47.072
Eventually, my husband
finished his PhD,

00:14:47.072 --> 00:14:48.572
we moved to Boston
for his postdoc,

00:14:48.572 --> 00:14:50.762
and Felix started
at a special school.

00:14:50.762 --> 00:14:53.552
For the first time since
that night in Indiana,

00:14:53.552 --> 00:14:57.182
I considered going
back to finish my PhD,

00:14:57.182 --> 00:15:02.882
except physics wasn't the love
of my life anymore, Felix was.

00:15:02.882 --> 00:15:06.092
Alas, not everyone found
that as poetic as I did,

00:15:06.092 --> 00:15:08.732
and I was routinely
rejected from every position

00:15:08.732 --> 00:15:10.202
that I applied to.

00:15:10.202 --> 00:15:13.472
Then, while reading an
Oprah magazine my mother

00:15:13.472 --> 00:15:17.312
had left in our bathroom, I
learned about the Media Lab,

00:15:17.312 --> 00:15:22.682
and I knew I'd found it, the
academic island of misfit toys.

00:15:22.682 --> 00:15:26.192
Finally, I would fit in.

00:15:26.192 --> 00:15:29.672
Graduate school has been
this winding, wild ride

00:15:29.672 --> 00:15:31.712
across disciplines,
trying to figure out

00:15:31.712 --> 00:15:36.392
the fundamentals necessary to
build a whole field of study

00:15:36.392 --> 00:15:39.682
around individuals with severe
intellectual disabilities,

00:15:39.682 --> 00:15:42.042
non-verbal or minimally
verbal communication,

00:15:42.042 --> 00:15:44.942
and multiple diagnoses,
including autism.

00:15:44.942 --> 00:15:47.882
I built devices, sensors,
algorithms, ran studies.

00:15:47.882 --> 00:15:50.072
Eventually, I found my
way to neuroscience,

00:15:50.072 --> 00:15:52.472
and began working with
members of the Gabrieli lab

00:15:52.472 --> 00:15:55.802
on a project to study the
neural basis of affinities,

00:15:55.802 --> 00:15:59.192
those deep, passionate interests
that individuals with autism,

00:15:59.192 --> 00:16:02.242
and indeed all of
us, often have.

00:16:02.242 --> 00:16:06.072
Felix's world
revolves around music.

00:16:06.072 --> 00:16:07.652
It's the way we
connect with him.

00:16:07.652 --> 00:16:10.858
It's the way he
regulates and socializes.

00:16:10.858 --> 00:16:13.172
And the greatest way
to make him laugh

00:16:13.172 --> 00:16:16.982
is a glissando, like Wile E.
Coyote falling off a cliff.

00:16:16.982 --> 00:16:17.922
"Kerr-splat."

00:16:20.582 --> 00:16:23.642
So instead of asking how
to minimize this love

00:16:23.642 --> 00:16:27.482
or how it interferes with
his life, we lean in.

00:16:27.482 --> 00:16:31.922
We ask how these affinities can
enhance or strengthen skills.

00:16:31.922 --> 00:16:35.022
My research is based
on what people can do,

00:16:35.022 --> 00:16:37.832
how they thrive, not the
ways in which they're

00:16:37.832 --> 00:16:40.074
deficient or lacking.

00:16:40.074 --> 00:16:42.032
Another project studies
nonverbal and minimally

00:16:42.032 --> 00:16:46.322
verbal individuals from
vocalizations, like mm, ah, ah.

00:16:46.322 --> 00:16:49.262
These are non-speech
but deeply informative

00:16:49.262 --> 00:16:50.905
modes of communication.

00:16:50.905 --> 00:16:52.322
We've built an
end-to-end pipeline

00:16:52.322 --> 00:16:55.922
to capture these vocalizations
in the wild, machine learning

00:16:55.922 --> 00:16:57.842
algorithms to classify
them, and we're

00:16:57.842 --> 00:17:00.572
developing a tool that shares
the meaning behind these sounds

00:17:00.572 --> 00:17:03.062
in real time to enhance
the communication

00:17:03.062 --> 00:17:06.482
between non-verbal
individuals and the world.

00:17:06.482 --> 00:17:09.692
We're trying to change the
question from why don't they

00:17:09.692 --> 00:17:14.012
speak to how do
they communicate.

00:17:14.012 --> 00:17:15.002
Here's the thing.

00:17:15.002 --> 00:17:18.342
There is a lot that
Felix can't do.

00:17:18.342 --> 00:17:22.112
But there's also so
much that he can.

00:17:22.112 --> 00:17:25.962
In life, we often don't get
to choose the path we're on,

00:17:25.962 --> 00:17:28.727
but we do get to
choose what we're

00:17:28.727 --> 00:17:29.852
going to do on the journey.

00:17:33.502 --> 00:17:35.252
KIRSTY BENNETT: Thank
you so much, Kristy,

00:17:35.252 --> 00:17:37.172
for that beautiful
story, and thank

00:17:37.172 --> 00:17:40.742
you so much for introducing
us all to Felix, and for being

00:17:40.742 --> 00:17:43.362
the sort of person who
faces a problem head-on

00:17:43.362 --> 00:17:45.572
and with so much love.

00:17:45.572 --> 00:17:49.232
Please welcome our next
storyteller, Mahdi Ramadan.

00:17:49.232 --> 00:17:51.872
When I asked him what he would
stockpile if he could go back

00:17:51.872 --> 00:17:53.687
in time to March,
he said something

00:17:53.687 --> 00:17:56.312
that I think we all would love
a little bit more of these days,

00:17:56.312 --> 00:17:57.572
Clorox wipes.

00:17:57.572 --> 00:18:00.452
Please welcome Mahdi.

00:18:00.452 --> 00:18:03.452
MAHDI RAMADAN: Thank you.

00:18:03.452 --> 00:18:05.552
My story begins
with my mom, sister,

00:18:05.552 --> 00:18:09.152
and I being evacuated by US
Marines from war-torn Lebanon

00:18:09.152 --> 00:18:10.802
to Cyprus.

00:18:10.802 --> 00:18:12.932
I remember finding a
cotton candy machine

00:18:12.932 --> 00:18:16.592
on the battleship we were on,
and just for a fleeting moment,

00:18:16.592 --> 00:18:19.172
I could enjoy being a kid.

00:18:19.172 --> 00:18:22.482
Not a kid terrified of
what he's just experienced,

00:18:22.482 --> 00:18:25.542
not a kid terrified
of what lies ahead.

00:18:25.542 --> 00:18:30.072
After five long days, we finally
arrived in the Seattle Airport,

00:18:30.072 --> 00:18:32.022
and a strong smell
of coffee welcomed us

00:18:32.022 --> 00:18:36.182
to our new home and
our new way of life.

00:18:36.182 --> 00:18:39.222
Now, this might seem
like a triumphant moment,

00:18:39.222 --> 00:18:43.202
that now we're saved, but
it's not always that simple.

00:18:43.202 --> 00:18:47.012
As we made our way home, I
remember a mix of emotion.

00:18:47.012 --> 00:18:51.542
We were joyful and relieved,
but at the same time nervous.

00:18:51.542 --> 00:18:55.362
Are we going to be OK here?

00:18:55.362 --> 00:18:58.692
With sudden change comes
sudden maturity, and even

00:18:58.692 --> 00:19:00.942
at a young age of
11 years old, I

00:19:00.942 --> 00:19:03.702
realized I had opportunities
here in the United States

00:19:03.702 --> 00:19:05.982
I didn't have back in Lebanon.

00:19:05.982 --> 00:19:10.062
I was absolutely fascinated
by the brain and mathematics,

00:19:10.062 --> 00:19:13.182
and I dreamt that, one day,
I want to go to college

00:19:13.182 --> 00:19:15.372
and I want to become
a neuroscientist, just

00:19:15.372 --> 00:19:18.972
like my hero, Oliver Sacks.

00:19:18.972 --> 00:19:21.702
As we settled into
life in Seattle,

00:19:21.702 --> 00:19:26.502
everything seemed bright, but
then one day, our foundation

00:19:26.502 --> 00:19:29.052
came crashing down.

00:19:29.052 --> 00:19:31.842
My dad had his
second heart attack,

00:19:31.842 --> 00:19:34.482
but this time it was different.

00:19:34.482 --> 00:19:37.072
This time, he lost his job.

00:19:37.072 --> 00:19:39.492
Medical bills piled up.

00:19:39.492 --> 00:19:43.692
We actually had to relocate from
our home to government housing

00:19:43.692 --> 00:19:46.002
to afford rent.

00:19:46.002 --> 00:19:50.142
I felt as if the ground
beneath us began to crumble,

00:19:50.142 --> 00:19:55.302
and I started asking
myself, am I going to be OK?

00:19:55.302 --> 00:19:58.352
Will I still be able to
attend college one day?

00:19:58.352 --> 00:20:01.042
I'm still going to become
a neuroscientist, right?

00:20:03.872 --> 00:20:06.872
As the years passed by, my
dream of attending college

00:20:06.872 --> 00:20:11.132
became more like a
figment of my imagination.

00:20:11.132 --> 00:20:15.882
I started noticing a growing gap
between myself and my friends.

00:20:15.882 --> 00:20:18.702
While they, during their
summers, went to summer camps

00:20:18.702 --> 00:20:23.202
for robotics, coding, science
and sports, I stayed home.

00:20:23.202 --> 00:20:25.452
While they received new
computers and video game

00:20:25.452 --> 00:20:29.872
consoles for their
birthdays, I did not.

00:20:29.872 --> 00:20:31.762
While they started
touring college campuses

00:20:31.762 --> 00:20:34.252
with their families and getting
private tutors to help them

00:20:34.252 --> 00:20:37.642
with the SATs and
personal statements,

00:20:37.642 --> 00:20:42.082
my family could barely afford
a car to get around with.

00:20:42.082 --> 00:20:45.412
At this time, I was nearing the
end of my high school years,

00:20:45.412 --> 00:20:49.092
and so I had to face my
reality and make a decision.

00:20:49.092 --> 00:20:51.762
Is college still a
possibility for me,

00:20:51.762 --> 00:20:54.642
or should I pursue something
more practical and get a job

00:20:54.642 --> 00:20:57.742
and support my
family financially?

00:20:57.742 --> 00:21:00.102
And I had discussions with
my family about college,

00:21:00.102 --> 00:21:03.432
and it was full of
uncertainty, questions like,

00:21:03.432 --> 00:21:06.432
even if I get into college,
who is going to pay for it?

00:21:06.432 --> 00:21:10.572
What are these scholarships,
and are they a scam?

00:21:10.572 --> 00:21:12.042
Why neuroscience?

00:21:12.042 --> 00:21:14.322
Maybe I could pick
something easier.

00:21:14.322 --> 00:21:18.442
What if this whole college
thing, this whole university,

00:21:18.442 --> 00:21:20.412
this whole academia,
science thing

00:21:20.412 --> 00:21:24.982
is just not made for
families like us?

00:21:24.982 --> 00:21:28.112
A pivotal moment came when
one of our family friends,

00:21:28.112 --> 00:21:31.352
Laura, who had known me
since I was two years old,

00:21:31.352 --> 00:21:34.352
and knew how bad I wanted
to go to college, basically

00:21:34.352 --> 00:21:37.712
forced me to apply for a
high school summer research

00:21:37.712 --> 00:21:41.402
internship at the
University of Washington.

00:21:41.402 --> 00:21:45.112
I was so sure that I wouldn't
get into the internship.

00:21:45.112 --> 00:21:47.602
Why pick me out of all the kids?

00:21:47.602 --> 00:21:52.082
But to my surprise,
I got chosen.

00:21:52.082 --> 00:21:54.652
The feeling that somebody
out there actually

00:21:54.652 --> 00:21:57.022
saw potential in me, even
though, at that time,

00:21:57.022 --> 00:21:58.932
I didn't see
potential in myself,

00:21:58.932 --> 00:22:02.652
is what inspired me to
actually go to the internship.

00:22:02.652 --> 00:22:06.142
Now, on the end of the
first day of my internship,

00:22:06.142 --> 00:22:10.402
I thought I had made a
huge, huge, huge mistake.

00:22:10.402 --> 00:22:14.362
The kids around me, they
were so smart, so confident.

00:22:14.362 --> 00:22:16.372
They already knew how to code.

00:22:16.372 --> 00:22:20.272
It's as if they've trained for
this moment their whole life.

00:22:20.272 --> 00:22:22.562
I just didn't belong there.

00:22:22.562 --> 00:22:25.242
I should go back.

00:22:25.242 --> 00:22:26.922
On the second day
of the internship,

00:22:26.922 --> 00:22:29.622
I learned I was
placed in a lab that

00:22:29.622 --> 00:22:33.282
dealt with a left ventricular
assist device, which

00:22:33.282 --> 00:22:36.912
is a system that you implant
for heart failure patients.

00:22:36.912 --> 00:22:38.472
And in that moment,
I really thought

00:22:38.472 --> 00:22:40.602
about my dad and
his heart problems,

00:22:40.602 --> 00:22:41.562
and that intrigued me.

00:22:41.562 --> 00:22:44.202
What is this device,
and how could I ever

00:22:44.202 --> 00:22:46.912
contribute to this device
and to these people?

00:22:46.912 --> 00:22:50.572
And that's what pushed me
to go in the third day.

00:22:50.572 --> 00:22:53.092
On the third day, I was
given my first task, which

00:22:53.092 --> 00:22:55.852
was to code up a
small piece of code

00:22:55.852 --> 00:22:58.762
to simulate the heat properties
of one of the components that

00:22:58.762 --> 00:23:00.867
have to go inside
the patient, and we

00:23:00.867 --> 00:23:02.242
have to make sure
that it doesn't

00:23:02.242 --> 00:23:04.702
burn the tissue of the patient.

00:23:04.702 --> 00:23:05.752
I'm terrified.

00:23:05.752 --> 00:23:07.192
I've never coded before.

00:23:07.192 --> 00:23:09.922
I've never even opened
the program before.

00:23:09.922 --> 00:23:11.032
Why did they pick me?

00:23:11.032 --> 00:23:14.032
Why not just another
student who should do it?

00:23:14.032 --> 00:23:17.182
But at that moment, I
had to stop and think.

00:23:17.182 --> 00:23:19.232
What if I don't take
this opportunity?

00:23:19.232 --> 00:23:21.382
What would my life look like?

00:23:21.382 --> 00:23:26.842
What if I'm forever constrained
by the financial circumstances

00:23:26.842 --> 00:23:28.732
that were handed down to me?

00:23:28.732 --> 00:23:31.962
But in that moment, I
decided to take control.

00:23:31.962 --> 00:23:34.672
And it took me two weeks
to create this little piece

00:23:34.672 --> 00:23:38.512
of code, but in those two weeks,
I created that piece of code.

00:23:38.512 --> 00:23:41.662
And little did I know that
this little piece of code

00:23:41.662 --> 00:23:45.862
would create the coil design
for the component that, selected

00:23:45.862 --> 00:23:47.842
out of many in the
lab, would end up

00:23:47.842 --> 00:23:50.392
being used for the
first clinical trials

00:23:50.392 --> 00:23:52.622
the system underwent.

00:23:52.622 --> 00:23:55.472
And not only that, but
it turned out later on

00:23:55.472 --> 00:23:59.702
that my coil design was as
close to optimal as it could be.

00:23:59.702 --> 00:24:03.432
And this is the first time that
I felt the ground solidify,

00:24:03.432 --> 00:24:05.942
and that the first step
I took in this path

00:24:05.942 --> 00:24:07.952
wasn't muddy, that
it didn't sink me

00:24:07.952 --> 00:24:09.882
and I didn't make
a fool of myself,

00:24:09.882 --> 00:24:12.422
that actually this is
a path that I can take,

00:24:12.422 --> 00:24:14.882
and that I want to take.

00:24:14.882 --> 00:24:16.682
I could go back to
my family and say

00:24:16.682 --> 00:24:19.382
that I have a contribution and
impact to make in the world,

00:24:19.382 --> 00:24:21.932
and that I can do
it, and that college

00:24:21.932 --> 00:24:23.842
might just be the thing for me.

00:24:23.842 --> 00:24:30.932
Inspired, I applied and got into
the University of Washington,

00:24:30.932 --> 00:24:34.832
took part of ever more
computational pieces of work

00:24:34.832 --> 00:24:39.662
and research, anything
from seizure predictions

00:24:39.662 --> 00:24:41.702
to models of visual cortex.

00:24:41.702 --> 00:24:44.642
And I'm proud to say
that, here at MIT,

00:24:44.642 --> 00:24:48.392
I've actually had the privilege
of teaching programming

00:24:48.392 --> 00:24:50.072
and how to code to
incoming graduate

00:24:50.072 --> 00:24:51.872
students in the department.

00:24:51.872 --> 00:24:54.332
And now, as part of
the Jazayeri lab,

00:24:54.332 --> 00:24:58.142
I study the computational basis
of counterfactual thinking,

00:24:58.142 --> 00:25:01.142
the same sort of cognitive
processes that went on in that

00:25:01.142 --> 00:25:05.072
pivotal moment when I
thought, what if I take this

00:25:05.072 --> 00:25:08.312
opportunity, but
what if I don't?

00:25:08.312 --> 00:25:10.592
And today, I share my
story for those of you

00:25:10.592 --> 00:25:12.182
out there who feel like me.

00:25:12.182 --> 00:25:13.922
And I want to tell
you that you don't

00:25:13.922 --> 00:25:16.832
have to be constrained by the
financial circumstances you

00:25:16.832 --> 00:25:19.209
were born in, that
you can follow

00:25:19.209 --> 00:25:21.542
the passions of your heart,
and the most important thing

00:25:21.542 --> 00:25:23.762
you have is not
money or connections,

00:25:23.762 --> 00:25:27.062
but the belief people
have in you and the belief

00:25:27.062 --> 00:25:29.362
that you have in yourself.

00:25:29.362 --> 00:25:33.442
And I'm here to say to
you that I believe in you.

00:25:33.442 --> 00:25:36.660
Thank you.

00:25:36.660 --> 00:25:38.077
KIRSTY BENNETT:
Thank you so much,

00:25:38.077 --> 00:25:41.032
Madhi, for such a
wonderful journey,

00:25:41.032 --> 00:25:45.042
from being on a boat with the
Marines to the Jazlab at MIT.

00:25:45.042 --> 00:25:46.402
It is an incredible story.

00:25:46.402 --> 00:25:47.912
Thank you so much.

00:25:47.912 --> 00:25:52.522
Our final storyteller for
tonight is Heather Kosakowski.

00:25:52.522 --> 00:25:53.992
And when I asked
her what she would

00:25:53.992 --> 00:25:57.262
stockpile if she could go back
in time to March, she said--

00:25:57.262 --> 00:25:59.512
and I [INAUDIBLE]
with her, too-- hugs,

00:25:59.512 --> 00:26:01.732
because she so
misses seeing people.

00:26:01.732 --> 00:26:02.842
Please welcome Heather.

00:26:07.642 --> 00:26:10.732
HEATHER KOSAKOWSKI: Have
you ever experienced hate?

00:26:10.732 --> 00:26:13.072
Not the haters gonna
hate kind of hate,

00:26:13.072 --> 00:26:16.852
but someone's deep loathing
directed toward you?

00:26:16.852 --> 00:26:18.982
When someone hates
you that match,

00:26:18.982 --> 00:26:21.472
their face becomes so
distorted with rage,

00:26:21.472 --> 00:26:23.962
they are unrecognizable.

00:26:23.962 --> 00:26:26.332
Violence is inevitable.

00:26:26.332 --> 00:26:29.542
And if you happen to be brave
enough, or stupid enough,

00:26:29.542 --> 00:26:32.902
to look into that
person's eyes, well, there

00:26:32.902 --> 00:26:37.252
are no words to describe the
intensity of hate-filled eyes.

00:26:37.252 --> 00:26:41.512
In that moment, the object
of hatred becomes just that--

00:26:41.512 --> 00:26:45.052
an object-- and ceases to exist.

00:26:45.052 --> 00:26:47.902
At least, that's
how it felt for me.

00:26:47.902 --> 00:26:50.652
My mom had the ability to
need me with one breath

00:26:50.652 --> 00:26:53.812
and, in the next, be filled
with such rage and utter hate

00:26:53.812 --> 00:26:58.582
that bruises were inevitable
and my life was in peril.

00:26:58.582 --> 00:27:02.962
How do you survive the
hatred of your own mother?

00:27:02.962 --> 00:27:07.072
Resilience, grit, perseverance.

00:27:07.072 --> 00:27:10.312
Those words have
marked my entire life,

00:27:10.312 --> 00:27:12.502
and they are not
words I am proud of.

00:27:12.502 --> 00:27:14.752
Behind those words
are a lot of pain,

00:27:14.752 --> 00:27:18.082
a lot of overcoming,
a lot of surviving,

00:27:18.082 --> 00:27:21.442
sexual, physical, and mental
abuse, homeless shelters,

00:27:21.442 --> 00:27:24.712
welfare, foster care, the Marine
Corps, systems and structures

00:27:24.712 --> 00:27:30.442
that broke me, failure
after failure after failure.

00:27:30.442 --> 00:27:34.202
And somehow, for
some unknown reason,

00:27:34.202 --> 00:27:40.912
I have still fought, still
persevered, still survived.

00:27:40.912 --> 00:27:43.342
My whole life, I only ever
dreamed of two things--

00:27:43.342 --> 00:27:45.592
going to college
and having a family.

00:27:45.592 --> 00:27:47.932
At the age of 26, I
started taking classes

00:27:47.932 --> 00:27:49.762
at the local community college.

00:27:49.762 --> 00:27:53.062
A few short months
later, I was pregnant.

00:27:53.062 --> 00:27:54.722
I dropped out.

00:27:54.722 --> 00:27:56.282
A baby.

00:27:56.282 --> 00:27:59.792
Messed up little me was
going to have a baby.

00:27:59.792 --> 00:28:03.902
How could someone like me,
that has never been loved, ever

00:28:03.902 --> 00:28:06.032
give love?

00:28:06.032 --> 00:28:08.732
When she was born, my
sweet little baby girl,

00:28:08.732 --> 00:28:11.462
I was filled with
such awe and wonder.

00:28:11.462 --> 00:28:13.262
In the maternity ward,
I would carefully

00:28:13.262 --> 00:28:17.222
swaddle her, place her in
her bassinet, and just stare.

00:28:17.222 --> 00:28:19.232
Her cute little cheeks,
her sweet little nose,

00:28:19.232 --> 00:28:22.352
her perfect little eyes, wrapped
in her knitted purple blanket

00:28:22.352 --> 00:28:26.522
and matching hat that nurses
came and went every few hours.

00:28:26.522 --> 00:28:29.752
One particularly peppy nurse
bounded in, oh my gosh,

00:28:29.752 --> 00:28:32.852
she is so sweet, I bet you are
just hugging her and kissing

00:28:32.852 --> 00:28:34.202
her all of the time.

00:28:34.202 --> 00:28:36.442
Then she took her
vitals and left.

00:28:36.442 --> 00:28:38.042
Kissing her?

00:28:38.042 --> 00:28:39.662
I can do that?

00:28:39.662 --> 00:28:42.422
She was two days old, and it
had never once occurred to me

00:28:42.422 --> 00:28:44.582
to kiss my own daughter.

00:28:44.582 --> 00:28:46.922
Right then and
there, I tried it.

00:28:46.922 --> 00:28:50.642
It felt awkward at first,
this learning to love thing,

00:28:50.642 --> 00:28:53.072
but also right.

00:28:53.072 --> 00:28:55.502
I started to learn all the
mom things-- baby baths

00:28:55.502 --> 00:28:57.992
and bouncing and burping
and diapers and swaddles.

00:28:57.992 --> 00:29:00.542
One day, when she was
just about six weeks old,

00:29:00.542 --> 00:29:06.032
she cried and cried and
cried, and there was nothing,

00:29:06.032 --> 00:29:08.662
literally nothing I could do.

00:29:08.662 --> 00:29:11.552
As she cried, I made all
sorts of promises to her.

00:29:11.552 --> 00:29:14.042
I told her it was OK, Mommy
was there, she was safe.

00:29:14.042 --> 00:29:16.022
I told her that I love her.

00:29:16.022 --> 00:29:19.352
And in that moment,
I knew it was true.

00:29:19.352 --> 00:29:21.362
A few months later,
the afternoon sunlight

00:29:21.362 --> 00:29:22.952
was streaming
through the window.

00:29:22.952 --> 00:29:24.782
With smashed avocado
and sweet potato

00:29:24.782 --> 00:29:26.912
stains all over the
counter, I looked down

00:29:26.912 --> 00:29:31.382
into this sweet baby girl's
eyes, into my daughter's eyes,

00:29:31.382 --> 00:29:34.572
and I realized, I want
the world for her.

00:29:34.572 --> 00:29:38.282
I want her to know that she
can do anything she wants.

00:29:38.282 --> 00:29:41.312
Looking at her, I realized that,
if I want her to truly believe

00:29:41.312 --> 00:29:45.992
she can accomplish her dreams,
I would have to accomplish mine.

00:29:45.992 --> 00:29:49.022
And so I set out on a
journey of self-discovery,

00:29:49.022 --> 00:29:51.024
of academic inquiry.

00:29:51.024 --> 00:29:52.982
I came back to the question
that has haunted me

00:29:52.982 --> 00:29:54.572
since childhood--

00:29:54.572 --> 00:29:57.792
what drives people to hate?

00:29:57.792 --> 00:29:59.712
A few years later,
I found the paper

00:29:59.712 --> 00:30:01.962
that transformed my thinking.

00:30:01.962 --> 00:30:03.612
I had already learned
that hate happens

00:30:03.612 --> 00:30:06.492
when we fail to see
the humanity in others,

00:30:06.492 --> 00:30:09.372
but this paper explained
the neural mechanisms

00:30:09.372 --> 00:30:13.512
that enable us to actually
see someone else's pain.

00:30:13.512 --> 00:30:15.311
Then it occurred
to me, if I want

00:30:15.311 --> 00:30:18.972
to understand what happens when
things go wrong, when people

00:30:18.972 --> 00:30:21.791
learn to hate, I have to
start at the beginning,

00:30:21.791 --> 00:30:23.291
before we learn to hate.

00:30:23.291 --> 00:30:25.271
I have to start with babies.

00:30:25.271 --> 00:30:28.092
Have you ever looked
into the eyes of a baby?

00:30:28.092 --> 00:30:28.794
Oh my gosh.

00:30:28.794 --> 00:30:30.252
If you haven't,
you need to try it.

00:30:30.252 --> 00:30:33.342
There is nothing more
heavenly in this world.

00:30:33.342 --> 00:30:38.262
Babies give and receive love
at any moment with any human.

00:30:38.262 --> 00:30:41.052
Not yet closed off to the
realities of this harsh world,

00:30:41.052 --> 00:30:44.351
human babies have a way
of innocently connecting

00:30:44.351 --> 00:30:48.732
with anyone that's willing,
and saying, I am here with you,

00:30:48.732 --> 00:30:52.842
and I see you as you
are here with me.

00:30:52.842 --> 00:30:55.482
It is powerful.

00:30:55.482 --> 00:30:56.922
So I set out to
better understand

00:30:56.922 --> 00:31:01.061
babies, the place where we all
begin, before we learn to hate,

00:31:01.061 --> 00:31:03.942
but are capable of immense love.

00:31:03.942 --> 00:31:06.912
How do babies see the world?

00:31:06.912 --> 00:31:09.462
My pursuit to understand
how infant brains support

00:31:09.462 --> 00:31:12.072
their minds is a new
dream, one I wouldn't

00:31:12.072 --> 00:31:14.442
have even dare dreamed that
fateful day in my kitchen

00:31:14.442 --> 00:31:16.272
so many years ago.

00:31:16.272 --> 00:31:17.892
It has been hard.

00:31:17.892 --> 00:31:19.782
So hard.

00:31:19.782 --> 00:31:22.072
I have cried a lot.

00:31:22.072 --> 00:31:24.532
I have lost a lot.

00:31:24.532 --> 00:31:27.902
But I have also shown my
daughter that not only can she

00:31:27.902 --> 00:31:30.372
achieve her dreams,
she can have dreams

00:31:30.372 --> 00:31:32.742
beyond her wildest imagination.

00:31:32.742 --> 00:31:37.092
You all, I am a
PhD student at MIT.

00:31:37.092 --> 00:31:39.762
Do you know how unheard
of it is for a foster kid

00:31:39.762 --> 00:31:42.912
to get a bachelors
degree, let alone a PhD?

00:31:42.912 --> 00:31:46.692
And yet, here I am, a
foster kid PhD candidate

00:31:46.692 --> 00:31:49.212
at MIT doing the
kind of research

00:31:49.212 --> 00:31:53.352
that only a handful of people in
the world have even attempted.

00:31:53.352 --> 00:31:57.432
Over the last few years, I
have held, comforted, swaddled,

00:31:57.432 --> 00:32:03.222
and looked into the eyes and
brains of over 100 babies.

00:32:03.222 --> 00:32:04.992
With every ounce
of my being, I want

00:32:04.992 --> 00:32:09.312
to know what is happening
in their mind right now.

00:32:09.312 --> 00:32:13.332
And with each baby that I
meet, I stare into their eyes

00:32:13.332 --> 00:32:16.872
just as deeply as they look
into mine, and make sure

00:32:16.872 --> 00:32:21.972
they know, just like my daughter
did, that I am there with them,

00:32:21.972 --> 00:32:24.432
seeing them, as they see me.

00:32:28.375 --> 00:32:29.792
KIRSTY BENNETT:
Heather, thank you

00:32:29.792 --> 00:32:32.192
so much for such
a beautiful story.

00:32:32.192 --> 00:32:34.742
It reminds me to spend
a little bit more

00:32:34.742 --> 00:32:36.997
time looking into
my baby's eyes.

00:32:36.997 --> 00:32:40.112
And thank you so much for
being so generous in sharing it

00:32:40.112 --> 00:32:42.332
with us tonight.

00:32:42.332 --> 00:32:45.542
Now, before our judges get
started on figuring out

00:32:45.542 --> 00:32:49.712
a winner, I want to give you
a quick recap of everything

00:32:49.712 --> 00:32:52.142
that we've heard tonight.

00:32:52.142 --> 00:32:55.982
In moving from Russia to Israel,
Ola lost her ability to read,

00:32:55.982 --> 00:33:00.642
a moment that shaped her
work as a researcher.

00:33:00.642 --> 00:33:03.822
Jill overcame rejection from
that dreaded reviewer number

00:33:03.822 --> 00:33:05.732
three.

00:33:05.732 --> 00:33:08.462
Kristy told us of a
complete career change

00:33:08.462 --> 00:33:11.762
to develop technology
to help her son.

00:33:11.762 --> 00:33:14.732
Mahdi overcame incredible
odds and self-doubt

00:33:14.732 --> 00:33:17.332
to pursue a dream of coding.

00:33:17.332 --> 00:33:20.722
After a promise to her father,
Shannon's circuitous career

00:33:20.722 --> 00:33:24.472
led her to the Boyden lab
and who knows where next.

00:33:24.472 --> 00:33:27.292
Dipon's late-night
Eureka moment in a lab

00:33:27.292 --> 00:33:31.132
cemented his ability as
an independent researcher.

00:33:31.132 --> 00:33:34.522
And finally, Heather told us how
she found a new lease on life

00:33:34.522 --> 00:33:37.642
after the birth of her daughter.

00:33:37.642 --> 00:33:40.612
And now, our judges,
Maddie and Tina,

00:33:40.612 --> 00:33:42.922
will be selecting
today's winning story.

00:33:42.922 --> 00:33:46.472
They've already ducked into a
magical behind-the-scenes green

00:33:46.472 --> 00:33:49.822
room for the next two minutes,
and while they're gone,

00:33:49.822 --> 00:33:51.892
you'll be able to see
some photos submitted

00:33:51.892 --> 00:33:53.422
by our storytellers
that illustrate

00:33:53.422 --> 00:33:57.422
each of our storytellers'
lives and stories.

00:33:57.422 --> 00:33:59.152
And while we can't
see you tonight,

00:33:59.152 --> 00:34:02.002
we have a chat function open,
and all of our storytellers

00:34:02.002 --> 00:34:02.962
can see it.

00:34:02.962 --> 00:34:05.452
So while our judges are
deliberating, please let us

00:34:05.452 --> 00:34:07.002
know if you're
having a good time

00:34:07.002 --> 00:34:08.542
and which stories you loved.

00:34:08.542 --> 00:34:10.462
And we'll see you back
here in two minutes.

00:34:14.454 --> 00:34:17.947
[INDISTINCT CHATTER]

00:39:03.002 --> 00:39:06.849
Welcome back to our
judges, Maddie and Tina.

00:39:06.849 --> 00:39:08.432
That took slightly
longer because they

00:39:08.432 --> 00:39:11.552
had a really unenviable
task of having to decide

00:39:11.552 --> 00:39:13.092
who would be the winner.

00:39:13.092 --> 00:39:16.112
So thank you so much, and I will
hand it over to both of them

00:39:16.112 --> 00:39:18.712
for their decision.

00:39:18.712 --> 00:39:22.459
MADDIE SOFIA: So that was
ridiculously difficult.

00:39:22.459 --> 00:39:24.042
I want to say, first
of all, thank you

00:39:24.042 --> 00:39:25.812
to all of our storytellers.

00:39:25.812 --> 00:39:28.632
It is not easy to tell
a story, it is not

00:39:28.632 --> 00:39:31.242
easy to tell a personal
story, and it is not

00:39:31.242 --> 00:39:32.922
easy to tell a story over Zoom.

00:39:32.922 --> 00:39:34.542
I will say that for sure.

00:39:34.542 --> 00:39:38.412
So we were judging on a
lot of different themes--

00:39:38.412 --> 00:39:41.252
we were touching on
themes, originality,

00:39:41.252 --> 00:39:43.122
and it was a really
tough decision

00:39:43.122 --> 00:39:46.482
because I feel like so
many of our storytellers

00:39:46.482 --> 00:39:49.492
today nailed it.

00:39:49.492 --> 00:39:52.392
But there was one
storyteller, in particular,

00:39:52.392 --> 00:39:57.752
that also did a great
job delivering the story.

00:39:57.752 --> 00:40:01.682
There was story structure.

00:40:01.682 --> 00:40:04.482
And I'll talk a little
bit more after this,

00:40:04.482 --> 00:40:06.482
because I don't want to
keep it from you guys,

00:40:06.482 --> 00:40:10.752
but we chose Kristy Johnson
as our winner tonight.

00:40:10.752 --> 00:40:14.052
And you know, as a person who
professionally tells science

00:40:14.052 --> 00:40:15.672
stories, I have
to say that Kristy

00:40:15.672 --> 00:40:19.872
did so many beautiful
things in which there was

00:40:19.872 --> 00:40:22.992
an emotional start, there
was a touchpoint for us,

00:40:22.992 --> 00:40:24.912
did we all see the
movies where, like, life

00:40:24.912 --> 00:40:28.032
flashes before the eyes, right.

00:40:28.032 --> 00:40:30.342
And then Kristy did a great
job of doing something

00:40:30.342 --> 00:40:32.142
we called signposting,
which is kind

00:40:32.142 --> 00:40:34.602
of leaving us little
things along the way

00:40:34.602 --> 00:40:36.162
that we're going to find.

00:40:36.162 --> 00:40:40.092
Big climactic moment, beautiful
resolution, and the story

00:40:40.092 --> 00:40:41.352
was really rounded out.

00:40:41.352 --> 00:40:46.032
And so I'm going to turn it
over to Tina to say a little bit

00:40:46.032 --> 00:40:51.162
more, but there were some
truly beautiful stories

00:40:51.162 --> 00:40:52.992
of resilience
tonight, and I just

00:40:52.992 --> 00:40:54.592
want to say thank you again.

00:40:54.592 --> 00:40:59.262
It was a very difficult choice,
but Kristy, congratulations.

00:40:59.262 --> 00:41:02.442
TINA HESMAN SAEY: Yeah, I would
like to echo what Maddie said.

00:41:02.442 --> 00:41:06.252
Every single one of
you told a great story.

00:41:06.252 --> 00:41:10.992
You all had turns of
phrase that I envy so much

00:41:10.992 --> 00:41:13.560
and would love to have stolen.

00:41:13.560 --> 00:41:19.452
And Kristy, what really
sort of touched me most

00:41:19.452 --> 00:41:22.662
was how you said that
you're not looking

00:41:22.662 --> 00:41:26.382
for the ways these
children are defective,

00:41:26.382 --> 00:41:28.602
but the ways in
which they thrive.

00:41:28.602 --> 00:41:31.102
And I think that
that is so important,

00:41:31.102 --> 00:41:34.432
and that is the
definition of resilience.

00:41:34.432 --> 00:41:35.702
So congratulations.

00:41:39.698 --> 00:41:40.656
KRISTY JOHNSON: Thanks.

00:41:43.642 --> 00:41:46.522
MADDIE SOFIA: I also want to
say for everybody, one thing

00:41:46.522 --> 00:41:48.802
that I always tell scientists
that come on our show

00:41:48.802 --> 00:41:53.812
is that your passion, your
vulnerability, if you choose

00:41:53.812 --> 00:41:57.232
to have that, comes through
more than any amounts

00:41:57.232 --> 00:42:01.162
of practice or lack
of jargon or whatever.

00:42:01.162 --> 00:42:03.502
And I will say that it's
hard to do that over Zoom,

00:42:03.502 --> 00:42:08.462
but Kristy, I feel as though
your emotion, your enthusiasm,

00:42:08.462 --> 00:42:11.362
your vulnerability, too,
really pulls people in,

00:42:11.362 --> 00:42:14.064
and people want to
pay attention when

00:42:14.064 --> 00:42:16.522
you can tell somebody is really
passionate about something.

00:42:16.522 --> 00:42:18.112
And so many people
did that today,

00:42:18.112 --> 00:42:20.404
but Kristy, I thought you
did a great job of that, too.

00:42:20.404 --> 00:42:23.782
So there's all kinds of
practicing and lack of jargon

00:42:23.782 --> 00:42:26.842
that you can do, but
really being truly driven

00:42:26.842 --> 00:42:28.762
by something important
to you is-- there's

00:42:28.762 --> 00:42:29.792
no replacement for that.

00:42:29.792 --> 00:42:32.782
So congratulations, Kristy,
and to everybody else, too.

00:42:39.992 --> 00:42:41.867
KIRSTY BENNETT: So
congratulations to Kristy,

00:42:41.867 --> 00:42:45.572
the winner of the first-ever
McGovern Story Slam.

00:42:45.572 --> 00:42:48.932
This very glamorous trophy
is on its way to you,

00:42:48.932 --> 00:42:52.532
as well as a giant
McGovern swag bag.

00:42:52.532 --> 00:42:55.712
I want to thank our judges,
Maddie Sofia and Tina Hesman

00:42:55.712 --> 00:42:57.962
Saey, for their time
tonight, and I really

00:42:57.962 --> 00:43:00.002
want to thank all of
you for taking the time

00:43:00.002 --> 00:43:01.532
to join us online.

00:43:01.532 --> 00:43:03.132
And finally and
most importantly,

00:43:03.132 --> 00:43:04.982
I want to thank all
of our storytellers--

00:43:04.982 --> 00:43:08.822
Ola, Jill, Kristy, Mahdi,
Shannon, Dipon, and Heather--

00:43:08.822 --> 00:43:11.762
for opening up and sharing
so much of themselves with us

00:43:11.762 --> 00:43:12.812
tonight.

00:43:12.812 --> 00:43:15.802
If you enjoyed the slam, please
visit the McGovern Institute

00:43:15.802 --> 00:43:18.932
website to learn more about
future events and other ways

00:43:18.932 --> 00:43:20.102
to stay involved.

00:43:20.102 --> 00:43:24.092
Thank you, and have
a great evening.

00:43:24.092 --> 00:43:27.442
[INDISTINCT CHATTER]

